{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["The reason for <b>which you slaughter in the evening on the first day</b> is that no meat from the sacrifice you made remain overnight until morning — the sacrifice being slaughtered in the evening of the first day among the seven days mentioned, which is the night, as he mentioned (Deuteronomy 16:1): \"who brought you out of Egypt by night.\" For in this passage he did not mention the fourteenth day or the slaughter of the Passover at all; rather, he mentioned the making of the Passover at night, meaning its eating and the eating of matzah with it. The reason is that the slaughter of the Passover and its offering on the fourteenth day is a matter of the laws of offerings, and he mentioned it in the section on the festivals (Leviticus 23:5), and did not repeat it here just as he did not repeat the commandments of other offerings — and also regarding the three pilgrimage festivals he did not mention here that we offer fire-offerings on them. But concerning the eating of the Passover and the matzah he did remind Israel here, and likewise there: \"you shall slaughter the Passover in the evening, at the going down of the sun\" (Deuteronomy 16:6) — there you shall make the Passover sacrifice in the evening, at the setting of the sun, for it is eaten at night. And he went on to explain this (Deuteronomy 16:7): \"you shall boil it and eat it in the place that YHWH will choose, and you shall turn in the morning.\" I have already explained this in the pericope of \"This month is for you the beginning of months\" (Exodus 12:6).\n\nIt is also possible to explain \"and it shall not remain overnight from the meat that you slaughter in the evening on the first day until morning\" as: from the meat that you slaughtered in the evening — that is, the evening preceding the morning, which is the fourteenth day. And he said there: \"you shall slaughter the Passover in the evening, at the going down of the sun, at the appointed time of your departure from Egypt, and you shall boil it and eat it.\" He mentioned <b>you shall boil it</b> without specification, for he had already explained there (Exodus 12:8–9): \"roasted with fire, and not boiled in water\" — if so, \"you shall boil it\" means roasted in fire, as is the rule."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "The verse citation Exodus 12:8–9 is given in the Hebrew as (שם שם ח ט), i.e., 'ibid. ibid. 8 9'; resolved to Exodus 12:8–9 based on context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:14:29.426236682Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}